hi there it's steve here uh the linguist  on language  uh i haven't done a video for quite a  while because i've been traveling um  with my wife uh 40th anniversary as i  said earlier  uh we were in portugal which was lovely  the people are so friendly and and  polite and it's just a it's just a sense  of richness being with those people and  and seeing the old towns and the culture  that they have and the food and the good  wine and it was great and now we're in  spain which is a little more dramatic  the people are not as  as polite and gentle they're a little  rougher  but they still are friendly and you  still have that sense of participating  in in another people's culture and space  we were in gerena which is well first of  all we visited catheres which is an old  medieval town in extremadura which was  phenomenal  just a sense of being  you know 800 years back in history then  we were in gerena which is a quiet  whitewashed  but very affluent and clean little town  in southern spain  near the border with andalucia  now we're in seville which is just a  groveling ant heap of uh  humanity i mean you just have this it's  so human you've got uh  you know old churches and people running  their markets out of them and building  into them and there's the streets are  full of people all the time and they  don't really make eye contact but they  don't run into you either and  it just moves and people are forever  sitting down and having  sandwiches drinking tea coffee drinking  wine eating  tapas of different kinds and it's little  shops and it's just  humanity and you know the net streets  are so narrow the woman who's ironing  in her balcony or bedroom or whatever  can just about touch the person across  the street who might be uh whatever  she's doing so it's a lot of fun  and it kind of brings me to  so when i'm here i'm motivated uh my  wife and i visited merida and we saw  some of the roman ruins there and people  were living in those roman ruins up  until 1972 so that they're just kind of  it's all part of their life there's a  continuity and i thought to myself jesus  i'm motivated to learn latin it's kind  of a pity  that that there's this dis  continuation between  the roman period and then  obviously there were the busy gods and  the arabs and everybody that came here  and then there it picks up again  uh but it really does go back to the  roman origins of people here and it's a  pity that the spanish is in latin so i  said to myself yeah we should have latin  at link  by the same token we visited the  wonderful phenomenal uh arabic  you know castles architecture here in  seville and so then you know much more  than the pictures we see every day of  terror you know terrorists or suicide  bombers you see that delicate  artistic culture and it was not just art  it was science it was medicine it was  agriculture that they had here in  southern spain far more advanced than  the  people in the rest of europe and so then  you're motivated to learn arabic  and of course while i'm here i'm i'm  totally into my spanish i i wish i could  take more books home i bought books i  bought this one here on the history of  spain which i'm reading and i even wrote  some notes on the back to help me  get through my  video and talk about the things that i  want to talk about  and really i want to talk about  motivation because i get emails from  people saying you know should we have a  four month old child should we hire a  spanish-speaking maid or should we put  our child in chinese immersion that age  you know while they're in the womb or  something  so that's one parent this people who are  tremendously stressed about how they  really want to help their kids learn a  second language  now i am quite sure that despite this  tremendous apparent interest in language  learning  nothing much is going to change most  these people are americans and most  americans will not learn other languages  not in the next 20 years that's point  number one so all of this  you know creating pressure on those  parents and on the kids to me is a waste  of time  uh  and unnecessary may not be a waste of  time but it's unnecessary  uh the second thing that struck me i was  in braga and i visited with the  professor there of languages at the mino  university language school and he was of  german origin or maybe austrian i don't  know which and he was lamenting the fact  that nowadays  language students are only interested in  spanish and chinese well english spanish  and chinese period no other languages  and i often get emails from people  saying you know  what's the point learning french do you  think there's a point in learning french  and so again i always answer it depends  on your motivation every language is  worth it and i think it's a pity if  if we try to reduce language learning  down to some kind of utilitarian  my kid has to have chinese because the  chinese economy is going to be so  dominant so i want my chinese my son or  daughter to have a better chance  uh  no  it's gotta come down to motivation  and then i was you know contemplating  because i always visit bookstores i go  to the bookstore here and the language  section in the spanish bookstores is the  same as in every other bookstore the  bulk of the books there have to do with  grammar  passing standard tests cambridge or  toefl or whatever it might be  and often like and then they had a book  here on you know in the spanish learning  section they had  phonetics spanish phonetics  intermediate a  uh intermediate b like how can you  possibly divide  the phonetics or the pronunciation of a  language into this beginner intermediate  and advanced  that's not how it works it's just a  gradual process  of introducing the language if you are  motivated to learn it and if you have  the attitude  which i referred to in one of my uh  videos as this this cultural  weightlessness that you can see yourself  as spanish here you are in in  you know sevilla you want to be spanish  you're in beijing you want to be chinese  if you have that ability to get outside  your own culture and if you're motivated  to learn the language the other stuff  just falls into place  and the more we put  these dry books of grammar in front of  people and say here's the rule get it  right  uh here's how you pronounce the diagrams  of the mouth and pick out 10 words that  begin with p and say them over and over  and all this kind of stuff  we are in fact  uh reducing the motivation in my opinion  now a very motivated person will learn  despite that  but people who aren't so very motivated  should be given more enjoyable ways of  learning the language as i as i always  say  so i'm just saying that the brain needs  motivation the brain needs to be  encouraged needs to be stimulated needs  to find new things  and in that regard too  you know there's always this discussion  between the crash and purists on the one  hand and the grammar teachers on the  other and i knocked the grammar teachers  a lot but the crashing purists say that  all you need to do is read and if you  get books or whatever reading material  that has only a small percentage of new  words each time that you'll graduate or  in the language yeah but you have to  read so much  in order to to get there with that sort  of very small increment of new words  that i'm not in favor of that and so i  say motivated be motivated by what  interests you and if there are more than  a few words you don't know not a problem  read them on the internet where you have  access to  uh  tools like online dictionaries i won't  mention link but that's also too  and  i do believe despite the krashenites and  whatever other proof they have that  when you  do study words that you have selected  from your own listening and reading  and when you try to produce the language  after when you're ready for it and you  get things wrong and you're corrected it  helps you notice and there are a lot of  things that happen in the language that  we don't notice  so  motivation by all means but also a  certain amount of attention to what's  happening happening in the language  and  yeah that was that the other thing i  wanted to mention that came up while i  was traveling was this whole uh issue of  uh of this canadian language portal that  was put up  you know i don't know what it cost them  to do that it's a canadian government  thing money is not an object  a bunch of information about language  learning again the language  establishment  shoving itself in the face of say under  the guise that they're trying to help  people learn languages they advertise  there's this  course that course and these translators  and these interpreters here we are the  canadian language industry that's not  what it's all about language learning is  about  motivation  here i am in spain i'm very motivated to  learn my spanish  and i would add too that the biggest  advantage in going to a country like  portugal or spain where i have been  is not that you're going to speak a lot  of spanish or portuguese because you  won't you know you buy a beer you have a  tapas you might find someone to talk  with you're listening to the radio or  whatever read the newspaper but mostly  it's stimulus and the big reason to go  to a country and expose yourself to the  culture is that it stimulates you to  learn but the learning you still have to  do it on your own and it's primarily an  input process  and if you go to my blog the linguist on  language you'll see a little piece that  i did on the principles  of input-based input-based language  learning and i better stop thanks for  listening
 

